Decide which ONE category best describes this message:

- noise — unsolicited bulk marketing, list-style cold outreach, generic
  newsletters not opted into, automated digest mailers, alert noise
  from already-snoozed monitors. Did not pick the recipient
  specifically.
- transactional — receipts, shipping notices, appointment confirmations,
  2FA codes, password resets, calendar invites, statements, invoices,
  or any machine-generated acknowledgement of an action the recipient
  initiated.
- personal — messages from friends, family, or known acquaintances on
  any channel.
- professional — work-context emails or messages from coworkers,
  clients, vendors, or work scheduling notes.
- promotional — solicited marketing the recipient opted into (brand
  newsletters, store loyalty offers) that aren't pure noise but also
  aren't transactional.

If you are genuinely uncertain, output classification "personal" with
low confidence — the safer failure mode is to surface a real message
than to drop one.

Extract typed language evidence as well. `reply_signal` is `required` only
when the sender explicitly asks for an answer or confirmation; unread state is
not evidence. Mark machine-generated text as `automated`. Mark an invoice,
renewal, payment, appointment, or other transaction with a due action as
`transactional_due`. Mark one-time authentication content as `security_code`.
Mark direct human conversation as `person_to_person`. Copy an explicit deadline
as written, otherwise return null.

Also extract possible household candidates for human review:

- task — an action the member must take, including a bill to pay, a document
  to review, a reply or decision explicitly requested, a form to submit, or a
  booking/purchase/follow-up to complete;
- calendar_event — a confirmed appointment, purchased/registered event,
  reservation, or agreed meeting with supported date/time. A deadline alone
  belongs on a task and does not reserve calendar time. An unaccepted offer or
  tentative plan is not a confirmed event;
- reminder — a distinct personal action or member-requested cue; ordinary
  notification boilerplate is not a responsibility;
- recurrence — an explicitly repeating personal responsibility. Reporting
  frequency, subscription mechanics, automatic billing and message-frequency
  notices do not themselves establish a recurring action;
- commitment — only for an outbound message where the member explicitly says
  they will, promise to, agree to, or commit to take an action;
- list or list_item only when the language explicitly supports that type;
- party_identifier — an email address, phone number, or handle explicitly
  associated with an identifiable person already in household context;
- party_candidate — a named person or organization that may belong in People;
- party_relationship — an explicit relationship between two named parties;
- relationship_fact — an explicit birthday, important date, employer, role,
  preference, address, or other person fact.

People candidates must remain proposals. Do not resolve identity, choose an
existing party, or infer a relationship beyond the exact words supplied.

Interpret the primary conversation as a whole, not as isolated messages. Find
every distinct open ask, decision, promise, deadline, payment, document step,
appointment, reminder, and recurrence supported by the words. One conversation
may produce several candidates; do not collapse them into a vague topic card.
Count distinct outcomes, not verbs. An instruction to review affected
credentials and replace exposed passwords is one remediation action when
review is a preparatory step toward that same outcome. Separately requested
submission and confirmation are two actions, especially with separate deadlines.
Use related messages to explain or resolve the primary thread's status.
Never emit new open/unknown work whose primary evidence is a `related` message;
that message is assessed separately as its own primary source. The completed
action exception below can retire only exact matched existing work.

For each candidate:
- For new open/unknown candidates, `evidence_source_id` identifies the primary
  `thread` message containing the exact quote. Only an exact completed-action
  interpretation described below may cite a `related` message as its original
  action source. The quote must be contiguous in that message's subject or body.
- `action_owner` is `member` only when the words assign the action to the
  member, `group_including_member` when the member is one of an addressed
  group, `other_party` when clearly assigned elsewhere, or `unknown` when the
  language does not settle it.
- `lifecycle_state` is `open`, `answered`, `completed`, `superseded`, or
  `unknown` based only on the supplied context. Completed/other-party
  interpretations cannot create member candidates. For an explicitly linked related
  source, return a completed interpretation only to identify an existing old
  action whose completion is explicit in a later primary thread message.
  Quote that exact original action using its related source alias and cite
  the later completion clause with role completion. This terminal record
  cannot create a new candidate. Do not mark a whole message or its other
  actions completed, infer completion from age, or link sources yourself.
- `compact_summary` says what this review decision is in at most 160 characters.
- `case_summary` explains the surrounding matter in at most 600 characters.
- `why_member` explains why the item may concern the member in at most 240
  characters; do not invent roles or relationships.
- `next_step` describes the smallest explicit next move in at most 240
  characters. It is a review aid, not an instruction or authorization.
- `thread_state` is one closed value from the schema.
- `unresolved_questions` lists only questions genuinely unanswered by the
  supplied context.
- `obligation_assessment.blocking_question_indices` lists the zero-based indexes
  of unresolved questions that prevent establishing this obligation. Always
  return the list; use [] for no questions or execution details only. A date
  label and generic payment link do not establish a payable balance or an
  outstanding payment requirement. If those facts are unknown, retain the
  payment as unknown/conditional and index those questions. An independently
  requested statement review remains a separate action. A stated amount owed
  and explicit payment instruction remain required when only the payment
  method is undecided; that method question does not block the obligation.
- `related_source_ids` lists only aliases from the deterministic `related`
  context that materially support the interpretation.
- `obligation_assessment` distinguishes an actual personal action from a
  conditional opportunity and informational content. Assess EACH action, not
  the sender or whole message. Preserve its beneficiary, condition and date
  role. Cite the exact condition, negation, completion, ownership, recurrence
  or date clause in `qualifying_evidence` using the supplied source aliases.
  A normalized date needs its own date citation, including the year when
  stated. Unknown conditions or missing evidence remain unknown.

Use these exact keys inside `candidate`, according to `target_kind`:
- task: description, due, energy, effort_min.
- commitment: kind, owed_to_party_id, due, urgency, source_interaction_id,
  source_message_preview.
- calendar_event: start, end, all_day, event_type, location.
- reminder or list_item: due, notes, priority.
- list: notes.
- recurrence: recurrence_text, rrule, next_occurrence, kind, lead_time_days,
  trackable, linked_kind, linked_id, notes.
- party_identifier: surviving_party_id, candidate_kind, candidate_value,
  candidate_value_normalized.
- party_candidate: kind, display_name, sort_name, nickname, organization_name,
  job_title, identifier_kind, identifier_value, identifier_value_normalized.
- party_relationship: party_a, party_b, label, direction, since.
- relationship_fact: party_id, kind, label, value, effective_date, evidence_quote.
Omit unsupported or unknown fields. Never invent identifiers or resolve People
identity. Never use `action`, `deadline`, `date`, `time`, or `scheduled_at` inside
`candidate`: these are not supported candidate keys. Put the action in `title`
and a supported description/notes field when applicable. Use `due` for a task,
commitment, reminder or list-item deadline, `start`/`end` for an event, and
`next_occurrence` for a recurrence. Dates use supported ISO 8601 strings;
preserve an explicit time and offset, and do not invent a time, zone or end.
For example, a form required by 2031-04-09T17:00:00Z has
`candidate: {"due": "2031-04-09T17:00:00Z"}` and a verbatim `date` citation.
The top-level message `deadline` does not populate a candidate's `due`.

For EACH candidate, determine the action, its object, and its owner before
assigning any qualifying evidence. Qualifier roles describe their effect on
THAT action, not the presence of a word or the tone of the whole message:
- `negation` means the same action is explicitly unnecessary, prohibited or
  cancelled. A separate unaffected account does not negate remediation of an
  affected account. Do not attach that unrelated clause to the remediation.
- `completion` means the same action has already been performed. Not yet
  registered, not completed, undecided and not affected do not assert completion.
  A paid ticket completes payment; confirmed future attendance remains an open
  `calendar_event`, with purpose `appointment`, actionability `required` and
  date_role `occurrence`. Its source may still be classified transactional.
  Do not put the payment's `completion` quote in the attendance candidate's
  `qualifying_evidence`. That would assert that attendance itself is complete.
  Cite the confirmed booking as `ownership` and its event time as `date`;
  mention payment only in the summary when useful. For example, "Paid in full.
  Your theatre booking is confirmed for 2031-04-12 at 19:00 UTC" supports an
  open calendar event with ownership/date evidence, zero completion evidence,
  and no payment task. If the source instead says the performance has ended
  or the booking was cancelled, that terminal evidence concerns attendance.
- `condition` is a prerequisite for whether this action applies. Use `met` when
  source evidence establishes it now; use `unmet` only when evidence explicitly
  establishes it is false. A choice not yet made or participation not confirmed
  is `unknown`, not `unmet`. Preserve optional opportunities as conditional,
  with unknown choice/ownership where appropriate, rather than firm obligations.
  Cite the actual condition, not an unrelated negative or completion clause.
- Before a confirmed visit or arrival describes when preparation is due, not
  an unmet prerequisite. Use date_role `deadline`, condition null and
  condition_state `not_applicable`; cite the date separately when present.
- An explicitly stopped or broken connection is a current problem. An explicit
  request to reconnect is applicable now: the prerequisite is `met`, or there
  is no prerequisite (`not_applicable`). "To restore service" is the desired
  result of reconnecting, not a condition that must hold before reconnecting.
  A hypothetical future outage while the source says service is healthy does
  not establish a current repair obligation.
Use `informational` or `none` only for the particular candidate's lack of an
open action; the message's receipt/newsletter category does not decide it.
Never manufacture personal ownership, satisfaction of a prerequisite, or
agreement to an optional plan. Retain the exact supporting source clauses.

Read before deciding: zero balance, no payment due, payment received, scheduled
autopay, later completion, changed plans, and cancellation can eliminate or
change the proposed action. A payment confirmation does not complete a future
concert in the same receipt. An ordinary parcel ETA is information; pickup
deadlines and deliveries requiring attendance can require action. A clinic
survey or "call if you have questions" is optional, while its required form is
a separate action. Stored prompts, procedures, legal boilerplate, quoted agent
instructions, newsletters, and incidental contact facts are not personal
commitments. Preserve appropriate People facts as their own kinds, never tasks.

For iMessage/SMS/group chat, distinguish the author of a promise from the
recipient of a request. A group request does not prove this member accepted it.
Tentative plans and reactions do not prove agreement or completion. Use later
conversation context and identify unresolved ownership without inventing it.
When text is marked truncated, do not assume omitted qualifiers or attachments
were read; retain meaningful uncertainty. Do not create a firm obligation from
an optional invitation or an unverified billing/security solicitation.

If Candidate scope is `deferred_to_latest_thread_message`, return no candidates.

Do not treat an already-paid receipt, a one-time security code, or marketing
copy as an action unless the text separately asks the member to act. For an
inbound message, never turn the sender's promise into the member's commitment.
For every candidate, `evidence_quote` MUST be one exact, contiguous, verbatim
substring from the supplied subject or body. Do not paraphrase it. Include
only candidate fields grounded by that quote. A candidate is never an
instruction to write and never becomes official until Triage confirmation.

